<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burundi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Burundi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burundi, officially the Republic of Burundi, is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">Great Rift Valley</a>, bordered by Rwanda, Tanzania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. A kingdom had emerged by the 16th century and was independent for more than 200 years. In 1885 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> was mandated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> as Ruanda-Urundi. Burundi gained independence in 1962. A coup in 1966 replaced the monarchy, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> took place in 1972; the 12-year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundian-civil-war">Burundian Civil War</a> followed in 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burundi.jpg" alt="Burundi" width="330" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burundi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Burundi is one of the smallest countries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, landlocked and equatorial in climate. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-rift">Albertine Rift</a>, the western extension of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">East African Rift</a>, and lies on a rolling plateau in the centre of the continent. Rwanda borders it to the north, Tanzania to the east and southeast, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> to the west, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a> along its southwestern border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a> is the political capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Bujumbura</a> the economic capital and largest city. The society is overwhelmingly rural, and the economy depends mainly on subsistence agriculture, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> as the main exports.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Twa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> and Tutsi have lived in Burundi for at least 500 years, though academics debate the early history and the nature of these three groups. The first evidence of a Burundian state dates to the late 16th century, on the eastern foothills of the Rift Valley. The king, the mwami, headed a princely aristocracy, the ganwa, which owned most of the land and required tribute from farmers, predominantly Hutu, and herders, predominantly Tutsi.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> mandated Burundi and Rwanda to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> as Ruanda-Urundi. In January 1959 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwambutsa-iv">Mwambutsa IV</a> requested independence and the dissolution of the union. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-for-national-progress">UPRONA</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-rwagasore">Prince Louis Rwagasore</a>, won over 80 percent of the vote in the first elections in September 1961, and Rwagasore was assassinated on 13 October. An estimated 250,000 people died in conflicts between 1962 and 1993, including the 1972 mass killings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> by the Tutsi-dominated army and the 1993 mass killings of Tutsi by Hutu, both described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocides</a> in the report of the International Commission of Inquiry for Burundi presented to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> in 2002.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melchior-ndadaye">Melchior Ndadaye</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> head of state, won the 1993 presidential election and was assassinated in October 1993 during a failed military coup, after three months in office. The civil war that followed lasted until 2005, and some 300,000 people, mostly civilians, are estimated to have been killed. The Arusha Agreement of 2000 was largely integrated into the constitution of 2005, and Burundi joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-community">East African Community</a> in 2007.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kirundi is the sole national language, and Kirundi, French and English are the official languages. About 85 percent of the population is of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> origin, 15 percent Tutsi and fewer than 1 percent Twa. Most people are Christian, with Roman Catholics the largest group. Burundi is divided into five provinces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burundi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>German East Africa covered 994,996 square kilometres, nearly three times the area of present-day Germany, and took in present-day <b>Burundi</b>, Rwanda, mainland Tanzania and the Kionga Triangle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Decolonisation was abrupt: the Congo became independent on 30 June 1960 and descended into crisis, and Rwanda and <b>Burundi</b> followed in 1962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of <b>Burundi</b> took shape in the late seventeenth century in the high, well-watered hills east of Lake Tanganyika, along the ridge that divides the Congo and Nile basins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The eighteenth-century king Cyirima II Rujugira is credited by tradition with stationing permanent armies on the frontiers facing the Kingdom of <b>Burundi</b>, Gisaka and Ndorwa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-rwanda">Kingdom of Rwanda · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The now pro-Hutu Belgians held a referendum in 1961 that abolished the monarchy, and on 1 July 1962 Rwanda, separated from <b>Burundi</b>, became independent under President Grégoire Kayibanda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Tanzania has also sheltered large numbers of refugees from <b>Burundi</b>, Rwanda and Congo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
